@namespace WebApplication.Components

@* The help panel: a drawer holding two tabbed groups of collapsible sections. Open state, active
   tab and expanded section are all C#, so the panel renders only what it believes is showing. *@

@if (open) {
    <div class="drawer-backdrop" @onclick="Close"></div>
    <aside class="drawer" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="Help" tabindex="-1"
           @ref="drawerElement" @onkeydown="OnKey">
        <div class="drawer-header">
            <h2 class="drawer-title">Help</h2>
            <button type="button" class="overlay-close" aria-label="Close" @onclick="Close">
                <Icon Name="x" Size="20" />
            </button>
        </div>
        <div class="drawer-body">
            <div class="tabstrip">
                <button type="button" class="tabstrip-tab @(tab == Tab.Settings ? "tabstrip-tab--active" : null)"
                        @onclick="() => tab = Tab.Settings">Settings</button>
                <button type="button" class="tabstrip-tab @(tab == Tab.Display ? "tabstrip-tab--active" : null)"
                        @onclick="() => tab = Tab.Display">Reading a run</button>
            </div>

@* Uses the labels the app shows, in the order it shows them. The previous text named controls
   that no longer exist (Heuristic Settings, Animation Delay, Execute Algorithm), described stacked
   canvases, and said annealing compares a neighbor with the best tour - it compares with the
   current one, which is what makes it annealing. *@
@if (tab == Tab.Settings) {
        <Disclosure Title="Instance" Open="true">
            <p>The graph the heuristic works on. <em>Source</em> offers three ways to get one.</p>
            <h5>Text</h5>
            <p>One node per line: a lowercase <code>n</code>, the node's number, then its <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> position, separated by commas, for example <code>n,1,4,2</code>. A node whose number or position repeats an earlier one is skipped.</p>
            <p>Simulated annealing, hill climbing and manual moves improve a tour, so they start from one: the order the nodes are listed in, unless a line <code>t,1,3,2,&hellip;</code> gives another. It may be written open or closed; anything that does not visit every node exactly once is refused with a message that names the problem.</p>
            <h5>File</h5>
            <p>A <code>.txt</code> file in the same format, so instances can be kept and reused.</p>
            <h5>Random</h5>
            <p><em>Nodes</em> nodes at distinct random points, x from 0 to <em>Width</em> and y from 0 to <em>Height</em>. Each node needs a free point, so the area limits how many fit.</p>
            <h5>Download graph</h5>
            <p>After a run, saves the graph as text in the format above, start tour included - the way to keep a random instance.</p>
        </Disclosure>
        <Disclosure Title="Algorithm">
            <h5>Nearest neighbor</h5>
            <p>From the start node, always go to the closest unvisited node; when none is left, return to the start. <em>Start position</em> is the start node's position in the node list (0 is the first). <em>Try every start node</em> runs once from each node and keeps the shortest tour; playback then shows each new best, not the steps of every run.</p>
            <h5>Simulated annealing</h5>
            <p>Each draw takes a random neighbor <code>y</code> of the current tour <code>x</code>. A shorter neighbor is always taken. A longer one is taken only if a random number <code>r</code> falls below <code>P = e<sup>&minus;&Delta;/T</sup></code> (the Metropolis rule), so while the temperature is high the search can climb out of a local optimum, and as it cools it settles.</p>
            <p><em>Initial temperature T<sub>0</sub></em> starts the schedule; <em>Estimate from the graph</em> sets it to the shortest farthest-neighbor tour minus the shortest nearest-neighbor tour. After each phase of <em>Phase length it</em> draws the temperature is multiplied by the <em>Cooling factor &alpha;</em>, and the run stops once it falls below the <em>Minimum temperature T<sub>min</sub></em>. Every draw is a step of the playback; the run ends on the best tour found.</p>
            <h5>Hill climbing</h5>
            <p>Takes only moves that shorten the tour and stops at a local optimum, where no move in the neighborhood helps. <em>Move selection</em> decides which improving move is taken: a <em>random</em> one, the <em>first</em> found scanning in order (next descent), or the <em>best</em> of the whole neighborhood (steepest descent). <em>Multi-start</em> climbs from one random tour per node and keeps the shortest.</p>
            <h5>Neighborhood N(x)</h5>
            <p><em>2-opt</em> removes two edges and reverses the segment between them; <em>swap</em> lets two nodes trade places.</p>
            <h5>Ant colony (ACS)</h5>
            <p><em>Ants m</em> build tours over <em>Iterations t<sub>max</sub></em>, choosing the next node by its pheromone times the inverse edge length raised to the <em>Distance weight &beta;</em>. The <em>Next-node rule</em> draws in proportion to these weights, or greedily takes the strongest edge (deterministic per start node, so the colony then explores few distinct tours).</p>
            <p>After each iteration the pheromone on every edge evaporates by the <em>Pheromone decay &alpha;</em>, and the edges of the best tour so far receive new pheromone (ACS, Dorigo &amp; Gambardella 1997, as in the thesis). <em>Stop early when all ants find the same tour</em> makes t<sub>max</sub> an upper limit.</p>
            <h5>Manual moves</h5>
            <p>Not a heuristic: you choose each move. Pick the <em>Move type</em> and its nodes by their numbers, the way an exercise is written. <em>If applied</em> shows the change &Delta; before the move is made, so a hand calculation can be checked; a combination the move cannot use is refused with the reason. <em>Apply move</em> adds it to the moves, <em>Undo last move</em> takes it back and <em>Reset to start tour</em> starts over. Applying a move at an earlier step drops the steps after it.</p>
        </Disclosure>
        <Disclosure Title="Run">
            <p><em>Run</em> (<em>Start</em> for manual moves) computes the whole run at once; the page does not respond while it does, which takes longer for more nodes. <em>Computed in</em> shows how long it took. If the instance cannot be read, the message says what is wrong in which line.</p>
        </Disclosure>
} else {
        <Disclosure Title="Playback" Open="true">
            <p><em>First step</em>, <em>Previous step</em>, <em>Play</em>/<em>Pause</em>, <em>Next step</em> and <em>Last step</em> move through the run. <em>Speed</em> sets how fast Play advances: Slow, Normal, Fast or Fastest (one step per frame).</p>
        </Disclosure>
        <Disclosure Title="Canvases">
            <p>Local search shows two canvases side by side: the tour before the step and after it (for annealing, before and after the draw). Nearest neighbor shows the tour growing, and the ant colony the best tour with the pheromone as line width.</p>
            <p>With <em>Highlight changes</em>, edges a step adds are green, edges it removes are red and dashed, and the two nodes of the move are purple (i) and orange (j). A rejected draw in annealing leaves the tour as it was and shows the edges it turned down, thin and dashed. <em>Edge lengths</em> writes each edge's length on it; <em>Grid</em> shows the coordinate grid. The text size in the header also sizes the nodes.</p>
        </Disclosure>
        <Disclosure Title="Tables">
            <p>The first tab is named after its table. <em>Distance matrix</em> lists the rounded edge lengths d(i, j) for nearest neighbor (highlighting the edge just added) and next to the moves in manual mode. <em>Moves</em> lists the moves of annealing (those taken), hill climbing and manual mode, with the tour before (x), the change &Delta; worked out from the edge lengths, the tour after (y) and its length. <em>Iterations</em> and <em>Restarts</em> list the best tour per iteration of the ant colony or per restart of a multi-start run. A table larger than 12 &times; 12 opens in full with <em>Show full table</em>.</p>
        </Disclosure>
        <Disclosure Title="Progress and calculations">
            <p><em>Progress</em> charts the tour length (and for annealing the temperature) over the run, with a marker at the current step. <em>Calculations</em> works the rules through for the current step with its own numbers: the change in length, the Metropolis rule and the cooling step for annealing, the global pheromone update for the ant colony. Every line follows from the one before as printed.</p>
        </Disclosure>
}
        </div>
    </aside>
}

@code {
    private enum Tab { Settings, Display }

    private bool open;
    private Tab tab = Tab.Settings;

    /* Opens the panel; the topbar button holds a reference to this component. */
    public void Show() {
        open = true;
        focusDrawer = true;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private ElementReference drawerElement;
    private bool focusDrawer;

    private void Close() => open = false;

    private void OnKey(KeyboardEventArgs e) {
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
            Close();
    }

    /* Focus moves into the drawer so Escape reaches it without a click first. */
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender) {
        if (open && focusDrawer) {
            focusDrawer = false;
            await drawerElement.FocusAsync();
        }
    }
}
